and welcome to CBS this morning we begin  with breaking news on the withdrawal of  all US military forces from northern  Syria there is growing chaos in the  region after President Trump's decision  to pull back US troops in the face of a  Turkish military onslaught for the first  time in years forces loyal to the Syrian  regime are occupying key cities formerly  held by us back Kurdish forces the  spiraling violence has also allowed  hundreds of Isis supporters to escape  captivity the US had about a thousand  troops in northeastern Syria fighting  Isis alongside those Kurdish allies of  the United States Charlie d'Agata has  been on the ground in Syria and just  crossed into neighboring Iraq well aside  from US troops were among a handful of  people who got the heads-up that the  withdrawal was about to be announced it  was like a two-minute warning we had to  pack up and get moving because the  situation on the ground was about to  change dramatically and quickly  America's allies hear that Kurdish  forces were taking a pummeling Turkish  troops advancing more quickly than  anyone had expected on the ground  Turkish back Syrian militias they even  filmed themselves executing Kurdish  soldiers and civilians on the roadside  dozens of civilians have been killed  more than a hundred thirty thousand  people displaced this is what this place  looked like until last week this whole  area under control of us back Kurdish  forces the Turks and their militias  drove all the way to a crucial highway  choking off supply and escape routes  now the Syrian regime is pushing forward  swallowing up the key cities of kobani  Qamishli and al-hasakah we were in  hosaka when the news broke about the  u.s. pull out we not only had to avoid  Syrian regime forces Turkish artillery  and air strikes their militias but also  Isis cells exploiting the newly created  power vacuum the militants had already  detonated car bombs in Qamishli and  hosaka from where we were based to the  direction we were heading  hundreds of Isis family members and  supporters had escaped from a detention  camp Kurdish forces were struggling to  contain 11,000 Isis detainees and dozens  of high-value prisoners were never  handed over to US forces despite  president Trump's assurances this  relatively peaceful part of Syria has  now descended into chaos and violence  now we've just crossed over into Iraq  from Syria because there are rumors that  the Syrian regime was about to take over  and close that checkpoint just gives you  an idea of how quickly the situation on  the ground has changed and how hostile  that environment was that prompted the  withdrawal of US forces Charlie d'Agata  reporting there thanks charlie
 

